{"text": "VERMONT STATE POLICE\nHeadquarters\n\nMemo\n\nTo: All State Police Personnel\n\nFrom: Colonel Matthew T. Birmingham\nDate: January 11, 2022\n\nRe: Motor Vehicle Enforcement\n\nOn December 21, 2021 the Chittenden County State\u2019s Attorney\u2019s Office issued a memo indicating that\nthat office will decline to prosecute all citations or arrests stemming from evidence gathered during a\n\u201cnon-public safety stop.\u201d\n\nRegardless of the position outlined in the memo, members of the Vermont State Police will continue to\nenforce motor vehicle statues impartially with an emphasis on the following:\n\ne Traffic enforcement is one component of the larger strategy to achieve public safety on\nVermont\u2019s highways. All members are expected to participate in this mission.\n\ne A data driven approach will be employed to focus traffic enforcement efforts in reducing\ncrashes. Traffic contacts should be an opportunity for members to engage with and educate the\nmotoring public.\n\ne The traffic safety elements of our Special Operations Unit will regularly assist local commanders\nin identifying trends that are pertinent to each station. High volume crash areas will be\nidentified. Troopers will be informed of relevant local information and educational talking points\nfor use roadside.\n\ne A focus on quality enforcement actions over quantity. Members are encouraged to continue\nusing appropriate individual discretion in your enforcement work. Members should reconcile\nthis discretion with the current crash data trends received at the barracks level. Assessment will\nbe made based on measurable impacts to crash reduction strategic goals.\n\ne Use of motor vehicle stop statistics (numbers of stops) as a means to evaluate Trooper\nperformance is antiquated, ineffective and can in fact harm our ability to reach our stated\ngoals. Quality enforcement actions focused on behaviors that are leading causes of crashes will\nimmediately end observed dangerous operation while creating positive interactions and\neducational engagement with motorists. Productivity will be evaluated through the collective\naccomplishment of strategic goals not by volume.\n\ne = The traffic safety elements of the Special Operations Unit will identify specific focuses to unify\ntraffic enforcement efforts across all 10 field stations.\n\nMembers should continue to forward charges identified during our traffic safety mission to the\nChittenden County State\u2019s Attorney\u2019s Office for review.",
